d and Higher-Order Derivatives" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050902 #edb/ref 1033 281 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050902 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "6fa843c3-0803-5725-a7d6-d567e4f1b216" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050923 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048335 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050923 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048621 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050923 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049070 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050923 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049842 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050923 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050088 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050923 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050312 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050923 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186054629 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050923 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186054630 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050923 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186054631 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050923 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186054632 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050923 #edb/ref 1031 "Subsets" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050923 #edb/ref 1033 50 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050923 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "723d1f9c-b696-511c-b5e5-88c045fe7609" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198156 #edb/ref 1069 "e-13171" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198156 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A = \\{n \\in \\mathbb{Z} \\: : \\: n^2-9 \\geq 0 \\}$\n\n- $B = \\{m \\in \\mathbb{Z} \\: : \\: m^2-9 \\leq 0 \\}$\n\n- $C = \\{q \\in \\mathbb{Q} \\: : \\: q^2-9 \\leq 0 \\}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198156 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1a2f8100-8f2e-5c6b-ade3-7192a914323f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198156 #edb/ref 1074 "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\nLet's start by analyzing the parabola $y=x^2-9$ for $x\\in\\mathbb R.$\n\nFirst, we find the roots of the parabola:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-9 & =0 \\\\ (x-3)(x+3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the roots are $x=-3$ and $x=3,$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](images/3c/3c2fc2f1eeb1d4e92f7933a408dafe7c3a216aa3d899f4025c71407140d47de1.png)\n\nTherefore:\n\n- The solution to the inequality $x^2-9 \\geq 0$ for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$ is \n\n$$\nx \\leq -3 \\quad\\text{or}\\quad x \\geq 3\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x \\in I_1 = \\left(-\\infty, -3\\right] \\cup \\left[3,\\infty\\right).$\n\n- Similarly, the solution to the inequality $x^2-9 \\leq 0$ for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$ is \n\n$$\n-3 \\leq x \\leq 3\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x \\in I_2 = [-3,3].$\n\nWith these in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite. If $n \\in \\mathbb{Z},$ the solutions to the inequality $n^2-9 \\geq 0$ are all the integers that lie in the interval $I_1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nA = \\{ \\ldots, -4, -3 \\} \\cup \\{ 3, 4, \\ldots \\}.\n$$\n\n- The set $B$ is finite. If $m \\in \\mathbb{Z},$ the solutions to the inequality $m^2-9 \\leq 0$ are all the integers that lie in the interval $I_2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nB = \\{0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,\\pm 3\\}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|B| = 7,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. If $q \\in \\mathbb{Q},$ the solutions to $q^2-9 \\leq 0$ are all the rational numbers that lie in the interval $I_2.$ Since any real interval of nonzero length contains infinitely many rational numbers, it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A$ and $C$ only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198157 #edb/ref 1069 "e-13174" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198157 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A = \\{n \\in \\mathbb{Z} \\: : \\: \\sin (n\\pi) = 1 \\}$\n\n- $B = \\{q \\in \\mathbb{Q} \\: : \\: \\sin (q\\pi) = 1 \\}$\n\n- $C = \\{x \\in \\mathbb{R} \\: : \\: \\sin (x\\pi) = 1 \\}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198157 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3ccf363e-d311-50e9-83b1-929ef1148554" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198157 #edb/ref 1074 "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\nWe start by finding the solutions to the equation $\\sin (x\\pi) = 1$ for $x\\in\\mathbb R{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\pi  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2m\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}+2m,\\,m\\in Z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is finite. There are no values of $m\\in\\mathbb Z$ such that $\\dfrac{1}{2} + 2m \\in\\mathbb Z.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nA = \\{\\}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|A| = 0,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $B$ is infinite. Listing the elements of $B$ for different values of $m,$ we have\n\n$$\nB = \\left\\{\\ldots,-\\dfrac{3}{2},\\dfrac12, \\dfrac{5}{2}, \\dfrac{9}{2},\\ldots\\right\\}.\n$$\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Since $B$ is infinite, and $B \\subseteq C,$ it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ and $C$ only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198158 #edb/ref 1069 "e-13175" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198158 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A = \\left\\{f^{(n)}(x) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d^n}{\\textrm d x^n}(e^{5x})  \\: : \\:  n\\in \\mathbb{N}_0 \\right\\}$\n\n- $B = \\left\\{g^{(n)}(x) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d^n}{\\textrm d x^n}(6e^{-x})  \\: : \\:  n\\in \\mathbb{N}_0 \\right\\}$\n\n- $C =  \\left\\{h^{(n)}(x) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d^n}{\\textrm d x^n}(2e^{x+3})  \\: : \\:  n\\in \\mathbb{N}_0 \\right\\}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198158 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "359028be-0c1d-5f5c-ace7-3d7aeb36d9e6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198158 #edb/ref 1074 "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{(0)}(x) & ={e}^{5x} \\\\ {f}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{5x})=5{e}^{5x} \\\\ {f}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5{e}^{5x})=25{e}^{5x} \\\\ {f}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(25{e}^{5x})=125{e}^{5x} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nA = \\{e^{5x}, 5e^{5x}, 25e^{5x}, 125e^{5x}, \\ldots\\}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $A$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $B$ is finite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{(0)}(x) & =6{e}^{-x} \\\\ {g}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6{e}^{-x})=-6{e}^{-x} \\\\ {g}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-6{e}^{-x})=6{e}^{-x} \\\\ {g}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6{e}^{-x})=-6{e}^{-x} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $B$ contains only two elements:\n\n$$\nB = \\{6e^{-x}, -6e^{-x}\\}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|B| = 2,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $C$ is also finite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{(0)}(x) & =2{e}^{x+3} \\\\ {h}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{e}^{x+3})=2{e}^{x+3} \\\\ {h}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{e}^{x+3})=2{e}^{x+3} \\\\ {h}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{e}^{x+3})=2{e}^{x+3} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $C$ contains only one element:\n\n$$\nC = \\{ 2e^{x+3} \\}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|C| = 1,$ which is finite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A$ only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198269 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048887 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198269 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048899 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198269 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049309 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198269 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050923 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198269 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186198784 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198269 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186198797 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198269 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287739 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198269 #edb/ref 1038 #ed